# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/006_switch_lowering_invariance.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_switch_lowering_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Switch results unchanged across dense and sparse lowering strategies at -O0, -O1 and -O2
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. No signed overflow: the largest value computed
is base + 1000000 with base equal to 0, and the largest case label is 1000000, well inside
32-bit int range on every target. The accumulator acc in grouped is initialized to 0 before
the switch, so every arm and the default read an initialized value. Every arm of dense and
sparse returns a value, and grouped returns after the switch, so no control path falls off
the end of a non-void function. There is no shift, no division, no pointer arithmetic, no
aliasing violation, and no object modified twice between sequence points. The printf calls
take a function call as an argument, but dense, sparse and grouped are all pure, read no
volatile object and modify nothing outside their own frame, so there are zero side-effecting
argument expressions. No dependence on padding bytes or on relative addresses.
The single volatile object v_zero is read once into a plain local and is never modified
anywhere in the program, so no ordering question arises between volatile accesses.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Widths are normalized: only int is used,
which is 32 bits on all four targets, and long is never used because sizeof(long) is 4 on
i686 and 8 on the other three targets. Plain char is not used, so the measured plain-char
signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64) cannot
be observed. No address, pointer value, jump-table address, sizeof or _Alignof result is
printed. No floating point is used. The choice of lowering strategy is deliberately left
free: a dense contiguous label set invites a jump table while a widely separated sparse set
invites a comparison chain or a binary search, and each compiler and each backend may pick a
different strategy and a different density threshold. That choice has no observable effect,
so the printed values are identical on every target at every optimization level. Selectors
are derived from volatile storage so no switch can be folded to a single constant return.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
dense[0]=100
dense[1]=101
dense[2]=102
dense[3]=103
dense[4]=104
dense[5]=105
dense[6]=106
dense[7]=107
dense[8]=108
dense[9]=109
dense[10]=110
dense[11]=111
dense[12]=112
dense[13]=113
dense[14]=114
dense[15]=115
dense[16]=-1
dense[neg]=-1
sparse[-1000]=1
sparse[0]=2
sparse[7]=3
sparse[100]=4
sparse[1000]=5
sparse[65536]=6
sparse[1000000]=7
sparse[42]=0
grouped[0]=80
grouped[1]=30
grouped[2]=30
grouped[3]=30
grouped[4]=20
grouped[5]=40
grouped[6]=80
END
